 \section*{METHODS}
 % ============================================================================
 
-The objective of this study was to identify the smallest set of metrics that detects the
-main types of error in a centerline graph. Nineteen metrics were computed under a common
-protocol, each on the curves that the two graphs trace, so that the node spacing of either
-graph does not enter the result. Four metrics that are defined on the nodes were computed
-alongside as a separate group. Ten error types were injected at six severity levels into
-reference graphs from five datasets, and the sensitivity of each metric to each error was
-quantified. Redundant metrics were then identified, and a minimal metric set was selected
-by greedy set cover.
-
-A centerline graph was represented as a pair $G = (P, E)$, with node positions
-$P \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times 3}$ in millimetres and undirected edges $E$, each edge a straight
-segment between its two nodes. $G_p$ denotes the prediction and $G_g$ the reference,
-$|G|$ the total length of a graph, and $\deg(v)$ the degree of node $v$, the number of
-edges that meet at it. A \textit{branch} is a maximal node path whose interior nodes all
