 \subsection*{Redundancy and metric-set selection}
 
-Selection of a minimal metric set required two steps: the identification of redundant
-metrics and the determination of the number of metrics required to cover all error types.
-Two metrics were considered redundant when their responses to all errors were similar. For
-each metric, a response profile was constructed from its mean shift at every combination of
-error and severity, normalised to the maximum of the profile. Pairwise Pearson correlations
-between the profiles were then computed. Because every responsive metric increases with
-error size, all profiles share a common trend, which dominates the correlation independently
-of what each metric measures. The mean across metrics was therefore subtracted at each
-combination of error and severity before correlation. Without this correction, the
