+Selection of a minimal metric set required two steps: the identification of redundant metrics and the determination of the number of metrics required to cover all error types. Two metrics were considered redundant when their responses to all errors were similar. For each metric, a response profile was constructed from its mean shift at every combination of error and severity, normalised to the maximum of the profile. Pairwise Pearson correlations between the profiles were then computed. Because every responsive metric increases with error size, all profiles share a common trend, which dominates the correlation independently of what each metric measures. The mean across metrics was therefore subtracted at each combination of error and severity before correlation. Without this correction, the correlation reflected error severity rather than agreement between metrics, and the resulting clusters were not reproducible across datasets. The common trend was taken from the main metrics only, so that the node-based metrics, which were added to the analysis for comparison, did not change the correlations among the others.
+
+The metric set was selected with the greedy set-cover heuristic \citep{chvatal1979greedy} applied to the sensitivity scores. The coverage of an error was defined as the highest score reached by any selected metric, so that a metric strong on an already covered error contributed no additional coverage. At each step, the metric yielding the largest increase in summed coverage was added, and selection stopped when the increase fell below $0.05$. Circular metric--error pairs contributed zero coverage, and the node-based metrics took no part in the selection.
 
