 \section*{DISCUSSION}
 % ============================================================================
 
-Centerline graphs are evaluated in different ways across the literature. The reported
-metrics differ between papers, the tolerance required by several of them is often not
-stated, and the node spacing of the graphs is rarely given. Our results show three ways in
-which these choices affect the reported score. First, no metric responded to every error,
-and no family of metrics was strongest in more than about half of the cases. The selected
-metrics therefore determine which errors an evaluation can detect, and an error to which
-none of them responds appears as a good score rather than as a failure. Second, two pairs of
-metrics behaved identically on all five datasets, and several metrics in use measure the
-same quantity under another name, so two reported metrics may provide only one kind of
-evidence. Third, metrics defined on nodes changed when only the node spacing changed and the
-reconstruction did not, while the same quantities computed on the curve did not change at
-all. As a result, two scores published under the same metric name may not measure the same
-thing, which limits comparison between studies.
-
-The first finding concerns the choice of metrics. The metric sets used in recent challenges
-were each chosen for a specific clinical question. TopCoW ranks on Dice, clDice and $\bzero$
-error \citep{yang2023topcow}. $\bzero$ error is appropriate for the circle of Willis, where a
-severed vessel is the most relevant failure, and our results confirm that a component count
-describes such a failure more directly than any other metric we tested. However, it responds
-only to errors that change the number of components, so the set does not report a pruned
-branch or a misplaced bifurcation. Our coverage analysis addresses this by identifying which
-metrics complement it. ATM'22 adds tree-length and branch-detected ratios to Dice and
-precision \citep{zhang2023atm22}, which covers the loss of airway branches well, but the
-80\% branch-detection rule, unlike branch coverage F1, accepts a branch as found whether the
-prediction traces it as one branch or as three.
-
-The second finding concerns redundancy. Some of it is a matter of naming rather than of
-design, because the same quantity has been reached from different directions and given
-different names (Table~\ref{tab:similar}). The completeness and correctness of
-\citet{wiedemann1998empirical}, the tree length detected of ATM'22 and the graph form of
-clDice are all the recall, the precision or the F1 of one covered length, Edge F1; on a
-graph, $\bzero$ \textit{is} the number of connected components. Edge F1 and the CAT08 overlap
-differ only in how they combine the two covered lengths, and they behaved identically on all
