+Centerline graphs are evaluated in different ways across the literature. The reported metrics differ between papers, the tolerance required by several of them is often not stated, and the node spacing of the graphs is rarely given. Our results show three ways in which these choices affect the reported score. First, no metric responded to every error, and no family of metrics was strongest in more than about half of the cases. The selected metrics therefore determine which errors an evaluation can detect, and an error to which none of them responds appears as a good score rather than as a failure. Second, two pairs of metrics behaved identically on all five datasets, and several metrics in use measure the same quantity under another name, so two reported metrics may provide only one kind of evidence. Third, metrics defined on nodes changed when only the node spacing changed and the reconstruction did not, while the same quantities computed on the curve did not change at all. As a result, two scores published under the same metric name may not measure the same thing, which limits comparison between studies.
+
+The first finding concerns the choice of metrics. The metric sets used in recent challenges were each chosen for a specific clinical question. TopCoW ranks on Dice, clDice and $\bzero$ error \citep{yang2023topcow}. $\bzero$ error is appropriate for the circle of Willis, where a severed vessel is the most relevant failure, and our results confirm that a component count describes such a failure more directly than any other metric we tested. However, it responds only to errors that change the number of components, so the set does not report a pruned branch or a misplaced bifurcation. Our coverage analysis addresses this by identifying which metrics complement it. ATM'22 adds tree-length and branch-detected ratios to Dice and precision \citep{zhang2023atm22}, which covers the loss of airway branches well, but the 80\% branch-detection rule, unlike branch coverage F1, accepts a branch as found whether the prediction traces it as one branch or as three.
+
+The second finding concerns redundancy. Some of it is a matter of naming rather than of design, because the same quantity has been reached from different directions and given different names (Table~\ref{tab:similar}). The completeness and correctness of \citet{wiedemann1998empirical}, the tree length detected of ATM'22 and the graph form of clDice are all the recall, the precision or the F1 of one covered length, Edge F1; on a graph, $\bzero$ \textit{is} the number of connected components. Edge F1 and the CAT08 overlap differ only in how they combine the two covered lengths, and they behaved identically on all five datasets. Definitions alone do not settle which names denote one quantity, since the branch count ratio and $\bone$ error are constructed alike and respond to different errors; the response profiles settle it, which is the argument for comparing metrics empirically rather than by inspection. \citet{citraro2020reliable} reached the same conclusion for road networks by a different route. Two metrics of one group should therefore not be presented as independent evidence.
+
+The third finding concerns how the metrics are computed. A centerline graph describes a curve, and its nodes are only where the extraction method chose to place them; two graphs of the same vessel can carry node counts that differ by an order of magnitude. Any metric that compares nodes inherits that choice. In the node placement test the prediction traced exactly the curve of the reference, yet Node F1 fell \new{to $0.50$ at a node step of $8\tau$ and Chamfer distance grew linearly with the step, and at the node spacing of a published method, TopoVST, a perfect reconstruction of a circle of Willis would score a node F1 of $0.54$}. A reader given such a score cannot distinguish it from a poor reconstruction, and since the node spacing was rarely stated in the papers we reviewed, a published score of this kind is difficult to interpret. Computing the same quantities on the curve removes the problem rather than correcting for it. The covered length of an edge is an exact interval, distances are measured to the curve, and path lengths start and end on points of the curve, so the node placement never enters the result: all nineteen metrics of the main set returned their perfect value at every node step. On graphs of comparable node spacing the node-based metrics measure the same thing as their curve counterparts, as the correlations show (Node F1 with overlap, Chamfer distance with ASCD), but only the curve versions give the same number for the same curve. The tolerance is recorded with every value, so that a score carries the condition under which it was obtained.
+
+These results suggest selecting metrics differently rather than adding new ones. A set should be assembled so that its members fail on different errors, which is what NetMets geom.\ F1 (or ASCD), endpoint F1 and total length do: they come from three different groups and together reach most of the coverage that any combination of metrics achieves. A set of this kind reports that the reconstruction is wrong, but not in what way, and this is the limit of what a general set can do. Identifying the failure requires a metric built for the quantity in question, and the structural counts serve this purpose. Their narrow response, which makes them unsuitable as general measures, makes each of them informative in a report: $\bzero$ error states that a vessel was severed or a false fragment added, $\bone$ error that a circle of Willis was closed or opened, the branch count ratio that the branching pattern changed, branch coverage F1 that a vessel is traced in pieces. We therefore recommend reporting a general set together with whichever counts the clinical question depends on, rather than choosing between the two. One error is detected by neither. A displaced bifurcation moves few points by a small distance, adds and removes nothing and leaves every count unchanged, so the general metrics average it out over the whole graph and no count detects it. Detecting it would require a measure computed locally at each bifurcation, and none of the metrics we tested is of that kind.
+
+To make these comparisons possible, we implemented the metrics in one evaluation tool, \ceval, so that they are computed under a single protocol and on the same graphs. Metrics that could not be computed are listed in Table~\ref{tab:not-computed} with the reason. Most need information that a centerline graph does not carry: a vessel radius, a designated root, a mask or several annotators. TOPO, the standard connectivity metric for road networks \citep{biagioni2012topo,he2020sat2graph}, would have needed an estimated 21{,}000 CPU hours for this study. The package, the perturbations and the sweep that produced these results are released at \url{https://github.com/EXAMPLE/ceval}, with a Dockerfile that pins the full software stack.
+
+The main limitation is that the errors were injected rather than observed, so we cannot show that they are distributed as real reconstruction failures are. The reference graphs are our only ground truth, so extraction quality is excluded rather than measured. \new{The ImageCAS references were reviewed by a single reader, and the reference artefacts we removed show that a reference can bias the measured sensitivity; references of other datasets were used as distributed.} The greedy selection is not unique and its stopping rule is a threshold we chose. The tolerance is tied to the image resolution rather than to the anatomy; a radius-adaptive tolerance in the manner of CAT08 would reflect the size of each vessel, but requires a radius channel that a centerline graph does not carry. One error, cycle disconnection, could be graded only on the synthetic reference, because the circle of Willis carries at most two cycles and the aortic and coronary trees none, so how metrics respond to it on clinical data remains untested. Finally, Dice and clDice are computed on the mask rather than on the graph, and lie outside what we measured.
+
+We release \ceval{} so that centerline-graph metrics can be computed under one protocol, on the curves the graphs trace, with the tolerance recorded alongside every value. Using it, we asked which of nineteen metrics in current use are worth reporting. Three metrics, NetMets geom.\ F1 (or, equivalently, ASCD), endpoint F1 and total length, cover most of what any metric detects on every dataset we tried, and structural counts should be added where a study depends on the quantity they measure. Metrics defined on nodes, Node F1, Chamfer distance, false merges and false splits, report the node spacing as well as the reconstruction and should not be used to compare graphs of different discretisation. One clinically meaningful error, a displaced bifurcation, was not tracked reliably by any metric we tested, and we have stated what a metric would need in order to detect it.
