+\begin{abstract}
+\textbf{Background} Stenosis grading, circle-of-Willis variant classification and airway generation counting are all defined on a centerline rather than on a voxel mask, and since 2022 a number of methods have reported the centerline graph itself as their output. How these outputs are evaluated is not standardised. In the papers we reviewed, the matching tolerance varied between studies or was not stated, and the node spacing was rarely reported, although every metric that compares nodes depends on it.
+
+\textbf{Methods} We implemented nineteen centerline-graph metrics\rev{, eighteen of them from the literature,} under a single protocol, each computed on the curves that the two graphs trace rather than on their nodes, with the tolerance recorded with every score. Four metrics defined on nodes were computed alongside as a separate group. We injected ten types of error at six severity steps into reference graphs from four clinical datasets and one synthetic dataset, measured the response of each metric against the realised size of the error, and computed every metric on pairs of graphs that trace the same curve with nodes in different places. \rev{Every perturbation was checked against what it was asked to do, metric sets were selected by exhaustive search with a bootstrap over patients, and the effect-size cap of the score was varied.}
+
+\textbf{Results} \rev{On graphs that trace the same curve, every curve metric returned its perfect value at every node spacing, while node F1 fell once the node step exceeded twice the tolerance, to 0.50 at eight times the tolerance, and Chamfer distance grew linearly with the node step; at the node spacing of a recent graph-extraction method, node F1 was 0.54 for predictions that trace the reference curve exactly. Every injected error changed the graph as intended. No metric responded to every error, and tolerance-based metrics saw an error only once it left the tolerance band. With metric--error pairs that read the injected quantity back excluded, endpoint F1 together with one curve-distance metric, ASCD or NetMets geometry F1, was part of the best three-metric set in every bootstrap replicate and at every effect-size cap; the third metric, junction F1 or branch coverage F1, depended on how the score weighted the separation between cases. The best set reached 98.5\% of the coverage reached by all nineteen metrics. A displaced bifurcation was graded within every case by ASCD and NetMets geometry F1, even below the tolerance, but separated poorly across cases (best score 0.620; 0.336 on ImageCAS); with a paired effect size, which treats each case as its own control, these scores were 0.854 and 0.661.}
+
+\textbf{Conclusions} Metrics computed on nodes report the node spacing as well as the reconstruction, so two such scores are not comparable unless the node spacing of both graphs is known; computed on the curve, the same quantities do not depend on it. \rev{We give a core set of metrics that did not depend on how the score was built in any setting we tested, show which parts of a recommendation do, and state what a metric would need in order to separate displaced bifurcations across cases.} \ceval{} is released with the metrics, the perturbations and the sweep that produced the results.
+\end{abstract}
