+\section{INTRODUCTION}
+% ============================================================================
+
+Clinical measurements such as stenosis grading, circle-of-Willis variant classification and airway generation counting are defined on a centerline rather than on a voxel mask, and a growing number of reconstruction methods now report the centerline graph as their direct output \citep{shit2022relationformer,prabhakar2023vesselformer,naeem2025trexplorer,topovst2026,palaniappan2026vesselpose}, following a broader shift towards explicit geometric output in medical image analysis \citep{lin2025pixels}. The graph also separates errors that a mask scores as nearly equal: a vessel severed in two adds a connected component to the graph, but changes the mask by only the few voxels at the cut. However, no standard exists for evaluating these graphs. The metrics in current use were developed independently in several fields, including vascular and airway imaging, neuron reconstruction and road-network extraction. Studies differ both in which metrics they report and in how those metrics are computed, most notably in the matching tolerance and the node spacing, so scores reported under the same name are not necessarily comparable. It is also unclear to what extent these metrics capture distinct properties of a reconstruction and to what extent they are redundant. In this work we compute these metrics under a common protocol and use it to determine which of them are informative enough to report.
+
+\begin{figure}[!b]
+\centering
+% TODO(2026-10-15): re-colour the Displaced bifurc. row from the repaired sweep.
+\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{introduction.pdf}
+\caption{Sensitivity of each metric (columns) to each injected error (rows). Red: error not reported; blue: metric tracks error size.}
+\label{fig:overview}
+\end{figure}
+
+Many metrics have been proposed to evaluate centerline graphs. For the purpose of this paper, we group them into four kinds by how they produce a number. \textit{Tolerance-based} metrics pair elements of the two graphs and count the pairs that fall within a threshold. These include node and edge matching \citep{shit2022relationformer,mayerich2012netmets}, coverage measures \citep{schaap2009cat08,lo2012exact,wiedemann1998empirical} and the overlap scores adapted to thin structures \citep{shit2021cldice,shi2024cbdice,rouge2024ccdice}. \textit{Distance-based} metrics report a distance in millimetres and need no threshold \citep{belli2019smd,liu2025ascd}. \textit{Structural counts} count features of each graph and compare the totals \citep{stucki2023topologically}. \textit{Geometric} metrics describe the shape of a single graph through lengths, angles and tortuosity. Path-based metrics \citep{vanetten2019apls,biagioni2012topo,citraro2020reliable} and the tree-matching metrics used for neuron reconstruction \citep{gillette2011diadem,zhang2021pyneval} use both a correspondence and a length comparison, and so belong partly to the first kind and partly to the last. A further group first matches the nodes of the two graphs and then compares how the matched nodes are connected, counting connections that are missing or spurious \citep{mayerich2012netmets,palaniappan2026vesselpose}.
+
+We identify three limitations of current evaluation practice. The first is that no single metric reports every error (Figure~\ref{fig:overview}). The four metrics shown there count different things: connected components, bifurcations, free ends and distance. Each one misses an error that the others report. The metrics a paper reports therefore decide which errors its evaluation can detect.
+
+The second is that there is no common protocol, either for which metrics are reported or for how they are computed. The choice of metrics differs from paper to paper: TopCoW ranks on Dice, clDice, HD95 and $\bzero$ error, together with detection and variant-classification scores \citep{yang2023topcow}, while ATM'22 combines tree-length detected and branch detected with Dice and precision \citep{zhang2023atm22}. The computation differs as well, so that scores under the same name are not the same quantity. Trexplorer Super accepts a node within 1.5 voxels on volumes with \SI{0.5}{\milli\metre} isotropic voxels \citep{naeem2025trexplorer}, while Relationformer sweeps an IoU range of $[0.5{:}0.95{:}0.05]$ over boxes built around nodes and edges \citep{shit2022relationformer}, which is a criterion in overlap rather than in distance. Vesselformer states no spatial tolerance at all \citep{prabhakar2023vesselformer}, \rev{nor does TopoVST \citep{topovst2026},} and Vesselpose leaves its maximum matching distance as an unassigned symbol, while noting that edge-wise F1 is sensitive to node sampling and matching strategy \citep{palaniappan2026vesselpose}. RefTr is the exception: it ties its threshold to the local vessel radius and averages over a range of radius multipliers, because a fixed threshold depends on vessel size \citep{naeem2025reftr}.
+
+The third is that the node spacing is a free parameter. A centerline graph is a set of nodes in continuous space together with the edges joining them, and nothing in that definition fixes where the nodes are placed. The spacing is chosen by the extraction method, not given by the anatomy, and two graphs of the same vessel can use node counts that differ by an order of magnitude. The two graphs share no lattice, so they cannot be compared element by element. Correspondence must be inferred from a matching rule and a tolerance, and the same pair of graphs can be scored as agreeing closely or poorly depending on how these are chosen.
+
+Each of these limitations has been addressed in part before. \citet{schaap2009cat08} built a consensus reference, released a database and fixed a protocol under which thirteen coronary algorithms were compared; the protocol was prescribed rather than validated, and it is defined on individual traced vessels rather than on a graph. \citet{zhang2021pyneval} compared four neuron-reconstruction metrics by displacing an increasing fraction of nodes, but only node displacement is varied, so what a metric does when the structure itself is wrong remains untested. \citet{decroocq2025benchmarking} injected a taxonomy of errors into tubular references and established which categories each metric detects. That study works on the segmentation mask, with mask-level error modes such as radius dilation, and the graph is never evaluated. \citet{maierhein2024metrics} formalised metric selection through a problem fingerprint and \citet{berger2025pitfalls} examined topology-aware segmentation; both reason at the level of masks and boundaries. \citet{citraro2020reliable} showed for road networks that connectivity metrics rank algorithms inconsistently because each is insensitive to whole classes of error, and answered it by designing three new metrics. Taken together, these studies leave three gaps. To our knowledge, no study has determined which combination of metrics is needed to detect the main types of reconstruction error, none has computed the metrics from these fields under one protocol and on the same data, and none has defined them so that the node spacing of the two graphs does not enter the result.
+
+The aim of this study was to address these three limitations. We implemented centerline-graph metrics from these fields in \ceval{} under a single protocol, in which the tolerance is recorded with every score and every metric is computed on the curves the two graphs trace rather than on their nodes, so that the node spacing does not enter the result. Metrics that are defined on the nodes themselves cannot be computed this way; they were kept as a separate group. This addressed the second and third limitations and allowed all metrics to be compared under the same conditions, which was required to study the first. We then injected errors of known type and size into reference graphs from four clinical datasets and one synthetic dataset, \rev{checked that each injected error was the error intended,} measured the response of each metric, and determined \rev{which metrics carry complementary information, how stable that choice is to the data and to the construction of the score, and which errors the metrics handle worst}.
+
