+The tolerance $\tau$ was set per dataset to the mean voxel diagonal, giving $\tau = 0.7$, $0.9$, $1.6$ and $\SI{0.7}{\milli\metre}$ respectively. \TODO{To check later: $\tau$ should be the mean voxel diagonal; the \SI{1.6}{\milli\metre} of AortaSeg24 was probably estimated on a subset of the training images (at \SI{1}{\milli\metre} isotropic spacing the diagonal is \SI{1.73}{\milli\metre}).} The tolerance therefore reflects the resolution of the image rather than the anatomy: a centerline cannot be located more precisely than the image grid it was extracted from allows, so a predicted point within one voxel diagonal of the reference was counted as correct. Trexplorer Super similarly sets its matching radius in voxels, at 1.5 voxels \citep{naeem2025trexplorer}. \citet{schaap2009cat08}, in the evaluation framework of the CAT08 coronary centerline challenge, instead tie the tolerance to the local vessel radius, which is better motivated but requires a radius channel that a centerline graph does not carry.
+
+The clinical data limit the study in ways that have nothing to do with the metrics. Most TopCoW cases carry no cycle, and none carries more than two. Cycle disconnection therefore usually removes the only cycle a case has, and all six severity steps give the same result: the median case shows one distinct realised magnitude, against five on the synthetic reference. An error that has only one size cannot be graded, so no sensitivity was reported for it on TopCoW. This is a property of the data, not a failure of the metrics. The aorta and the coronary arteries have no anatomical cycle, so cycle disconnection was not applied to AortaSeg24 or ImageCAS. \new{The AortaSeg24 reference graphs do contain small loops, in 20 of 100 cases, but these are annotation artefacts: the median loop is \SI{4.2}{\milli\metre} long and has four nodes, against \SI{102}{\milli\metre} for a circle of Willis. The ImageCAS references, re-extracted as described above, contain none.} \rev{The TopCoW and AortaSeg24 references were not audited as the ImageCAS references were, and these loops were kept.} AortaSeg24 is a single connected component in every case.
+
+Measuring how a metric follows the \textit{size} of an error requires a reference in which that size can be set freely, and no clinical dataset allows this. We therefore generated a synthetic dataset ($n=30$, one graph per seed). Every graph was built to the same specification: three components with eight bifurcations and two cycles each, grown from seed points, closed into cycles, pruned to the target bifurcation count and sampled at \SI{0.3}{\milli\metre}. The components are interchangeable, so deleting one of three, or severing one of six cycles, is a step of known size. The generator and its parameters are released with \ceval.
