+\section{METHODS}
+% ============================================================================
+
+The objective of this study was to \rev{determine which metrics respond to which errors in a centerline graph, and which small sets of metrics jointly detect them}. Nineteen metrics were computed under a common protocol, each on the curves that the two graphs trace, so that the node spacing of either graph does not enter the result. Four metrics that are defined on the nodes were computed alongside as a separate group. Ten error types were injected at six severity levels into reference graphs from five datasets, and the sensitivity of each metric to each error was quantified. Redundant metrics were then identified, and \rev{compact metric sets were selected by exhaustive search, their stability assessed by a bootstrap over patients and their dependence on the construction of the score by varying it}.
+
+A centerline graph was represented as a pair $G = (P, E)$, with node positions $P \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times 3}$ in millimetres and undirected edges $E$, each edge a straight segment between its two nodes. $G_p$ denotes the prediction and $G_g$ the reference, $|G|$ the total length of a graph, and $\deg(v)$ the degree of node $v$, the number of edges that meet at it. A \textit{branch} is a maximal node path whose interior nodes all have $\deg = 2$, that is, a path between two nodes of degree $\neq 2$; the set of branches is $B(G)$. A component that formed a pure cycle was broken at an arbitrary edge so that it yielded one branch. All metrics were computed on the graph, never on the voxel mask, and on the graphs as given.
+
+\subsection{Computing metrics on the curve}
+
+A centerline graph describes a curve, the union of its edges, and the nodes are only where the extraction method chose to place them. Two graphs of the same vessel can trace the same curve with very different node positions, so a metric that compares nodes reports the placement as well as the reconstruction. Every metric in the main set was therefore defined on the curve (Figure~\ref{fig:curve}).
+
+\begin{figure}[t]
+\centering
+\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{curve_metrics.png}
+\caption{How the metrics are computed on the curve. (a) The part of an edge within $\tau$ of a reference segment is the part inside a capsule around the segment, a single interval found in closed form. (b) Summed over all edges, these intervals give the length of each graph within $\tau$ of the other, $C_p$ and $C_g$, and from them Edge F1, Edge R and overlap.}
+\label{fig:curve}
+\end{figure}
+
+The part of a straight edge that lies within $\tau$ of a reference edge is the part of it inside a capsule around the reference edge: a cylinder of radius $\tau$ capped by two balls. A capsule is convex, so this part is a single interval along the edge, and its two ends are roots of quadratic equations (Figure~\ref{fig:curve}a). The union of these intervals over every reference edge within reach gives the exact part of an edge that lies within $\tau$ of the reference graph, and the sum over all edges gives $C_p$, the length of the prediction within $\tau$ of the reference. $C_g$, the length of the reference within $\tau$ of the prediction, was computed in the same way with the roles exchanged (Figure~\ref{fig:curve}b). The distance from a point on one curve to the other curve was computed exactly as the distance to the nearest segment. Means and percentiles of this distance along a curve were obtained by splitting each edge adaptively until the distance varied by less than \new{$\tau/10$} on every piece\new{; a resolution of $\tau/100$ changed no value by more than $0.001$}. Nothing in these computations refers to the position of a node, so splitting an edge into several, or merging collinear edges, leaves every value unchanged. \rev{Resampling both curves densely would approach the same values, but only as the sampling step goes to zero, with that step as a further parameter and a cost that grows as it shrinks; the intervals here are exact at any node spacing, so the invariance holds by construction rather than approximately.}
+
+\new{Eight metrics of the main set were computed on the curve instead of on points, as in their published forms: Edge F1, Edge R, overlap, both branch coverage scores, NetMets geom.\ F1, ASCD and HD95. Their published forms count or measure points sampled from the graph, and when a graph is evaluated directly these points are its nodes. The other eleven metrics were computed as published. APLS already places its control points on the curve. Junction F1, endpoint F1 and NetMets conn.\ F1 use only nodes of degree $\neq 2$, which the node spacing does not move, and the structural counts and geometric descriptors use only the branches. What the eight metrics would give if they were computed on the nodes is shown in Figure~\ref{fig:spacing-nodes} in the appendix.}
+
+\subsection{Metric definitions}
+
+The metrics were drawn from four fields that developed them independently. From vascular imaging, these were the coronary measures of CAT08 \citep{schaap2009cat08}, the skeleton-based overlap scores \citep{shit2021cldice}, the topological counts \citep{stucki2023topologically} and the average symmetric centerline distance \citep{liu2025ascd}; from airway imaging, the branch-detection measure of EXACT'09 \citep{lo2012exact} in the form ranked in ATM'22 \citep{zhang2023atm22}; from neuron reconstruction, the geometric and connectivity scores of NetMets \citep{mayerich2012netmets} and the node classes of DIADEM \citep{gillette2011diadem}; and from road-network extraction, the completeness and correctness measures \citep{wiedemann1998empirical} and the path-based score APLS \citep{vanetten2019apls}. The metrics are described below by what they compute rather than by their origin, because several apply the same construction to a different object. Metrics from these fields that measure the same quantity under another name, or that could not be computed on centerline graphs, are listed with the reason in Tables~\ref{tab:similar} and~\ref{tab:not-computed}. \rev{Eighteen of the nineteen metrics come from the literature; branch coverage F1 is our own construction.}
+
+\textit{Tolerance-based} metrics counted the part of one graph that lay within $\tau$ of the other, and therefore required $\tau$. \textit{Edge F1} was the F1 score of precision $P = C_p/|G_p|$, the fraction of the prediction lying on the reference, and recall $R = C_g/|G_g|$, the fraction of the reference found in the prediction. \textit{Edge R} was that recall alone, published as completeness \citep{wiedemann1998empirical} and, for airways, as tree length detected \citep{zhang2023atm22}. \textit{Overlap (OV)} pooled the two covered lengths, $(C_p + C_g)/(|G_p| + |G_g|)$, as the CAT08 overlap pools its point counts \citep{schaap2009cat08}; it equals Edge F1 whenever $C_p = C_g$. CAT08 uses the local vessel radius as its tolerance and an ordered correspondence along single vessels; $\tau$ was used here, and no ordering. \textit{Branch cov.\ F1 (80\%)} counted a branch as detected when at least 80\% of its length lay within $\tau$ of the other graph \citep{lo2012exact,zhang2023atm22}, and took the F1 of the detected fractions of predicted and reference branches. \textit{Branch cov.\ F1}\rev{, which we introduce,} took, for every branch, the longest stretch lying within $\tau$ of a single branch of the other graph, as a fraction of the branch length, and averaged it over the branches weighted by length, in both directions. Requiring one counterpart branch rather than the union of all of them is what makes a vessel traced in pieces, or two vessels traced as one branch, score below one. \textit{NetMets geom.\ F1} replaced the hard threshold by a Gaussian weight: the mean of $1 - \exp(-d^2/2\sigma^2)$ along each curve, with $d$ the distance to the other curve and $\sigma = \tau$, gives a false-positive and a false-negative rate, and the score was the F1 of one minus each \citep{mayerich2012netmets}. \rev{NetMets itself resamples each fibre at a spacing of at most $\varepsilon\sigma$ and takes $\sigma$ from the mean vessel radius; a centerline graph carries no radius, so $\sigma = \tau$ here, and the distance is integrated along the curve rather than sampled.} \textit{Junction F1} and \textit{Endpoint F1} counted a bifurcation (degree $\geq 3$) or an endpoint (degree 1) as found when one of the same kind lay within $\tau$ in the other graph, the node classes on which DIADEM and NetMets build their matching \citep{gillette2011diadem,mayerich2012netmets}. Bifurcations and endpoints are defined by the structure of the graph, not by the node spacing. \textit{NetMets conn.\ F1} \citep{mayerich2012netmets} paired the bifurcations and endpoints of the two graphs within $\tau$, each with at most one partner, reduced each graph to a core graph that joins two matched nodes when a path between them passes only through unmatched ones, and compared the core edges; the score was the F1 of one minus the false-positive and false-negative connection rates.
+
+\textit{Distance-based} metrics reported a distance in millimetres or a comparison of path lengths. \textit{ASCD}, the average symmetric centerline distance \citep{liu2025ascd}, was the mean distance along each curve to the other, averaged over both directions,
+\begin{equation}
+\mathrm{ASCD} = \tfrac{1}{2}\Big(\tfrac{1}{|G_p|}\!\int_{G_p}\! d(x, G_g)\,\mathrm{d}x
+                      + \tfrac{1}{|G_g|}\!\int_{G_g}\! d(y, G_p)\,\mathrm{d}y\Big),
+\label{eq:ascd}
+\end{equation}
+and \textit{HD95} was the 95th percentile of the same distances, pooled over both curves and weighted by length. \textit{APLS} \citep{vanetten2019apls} compared shortest-path lengths. Control points were the bifurcations, the endpoints and a point every $5\tau$ along each branch, as the original places them at regular distances along roads. Each control point was carried to the closest point of the other curve within $\tau$. For a pair of control points with path length $L$ on one graph and $L'$ between their counterparts on the other,
+\begin{equation}
+D(G_p \,|\, G_g) = 1 - \overline{\min\!\left(1,\; \frac{|L' - L|}{L}\right)} ,
+\label{eq:apls}
+\end{equation}
+averaged over randomly drawn pairs, with a pair scored 0 when a point had no counterpart within $\tau$ or no path existed. \new{The pairs were drawn with a fixed seed from the control points sorted by position, and each branch was walked from its end with the smaller coordinates, so that neither the control points nor the pairs depend on how a graph numbers its nodes.} APLS was the harmonic mean of $D(G_p|G_g)$ and $D(G_g|G_p)$.
+
+\textit{Structural counts} required neither a tolerance nor a matching. $\bzero$ and $\bone$ error were the absolute differences in the number of connected components and of cycles \citep{stucki2023topologically}, and \textit{Branch count ratio} was $\big|\,|B(G_p)|/|B(G_g)| - 1\,\big|$. \textit{Geometric} metrics summarised the shape of a single graph: total length, mean branch length, mean tortuosity (branch length over chord) and mean bifurcation angle. They were computed for both graphs and compared as absolute differences. Each describes a distribution rather than a correspondence, so a prediction can match the reference on all of them without a single point in the correct position \citep{maierhein2024metrics}.
+
+\textit{Node-based} metrics compare the positions of nodes and were kept apart. \textit{Node F1} counted a node as found when a node of the other graph lay within $\tau$ \citep{naeem2025trexplorer,palaniappan2026vesselpose}\rev{; Vesselpose first resamples both graphs at one voxel, whereas here the nodes were matched as given, which is what this group is meant to expose}, and \textit{Chamfer dist.} was the mean distance from each node to the nearest node of the other graph, averaged over both directions. \textit{False merges} and \textit{false splits} \citep{palaniappan2026vesselpose} paired all nodes of the two graphs within $\tau$, each with at most one partner; a false merge was a predicted connection between matched nodes whose reference counterparts lie in different components, or in one component with no reference path between them shorter than the connection plus $2\tau$, and false splits was the $\bzero$ error after the false merges had been removed. \rev{Vesselpose defines a false merge through the ancestry of the reference tree; the definition by path length used here is our adaptation to graphs that need not be trees.} All four depend on where the nodes are placed along the curve. They were included in the tables and the correlation analysis for comparison, but not in the selection of a metric set.
+
+\subsection{Node placement}
+
+To test whether a metric depends on node placement, every metric was also computed on pairs of graphs that trace exactly the same curve. For each clinical case and each factor \new{$f$ from $0.25$ to $10$ in steps of $0.25$ (40 steps)}, the prediction carried a node every $f\tau$ along each branch of the reference, which gives a curve of straight pieces, and the reference was that same curve with a node every $\tau/4$. \new{Branch ends were kept at every step.} Both graphs of a pair trace one curve, so every departure from a metric's perfect value reports the node placement and not an error.
+
+\new{For comparison with a real method, the node spacing of TopoVST \citep{topovst2026} was marked on these curves. \rev{Predictions of TopoVST, which we trained on each dataset and applied to its test cases,} \TODO{tracking step size, which sets the node spacing} place a node every \SI{5.7}{}, \SI{4.4}{}, \SI{4.9}{} and \SI{1.6}{\milli\metre} (median edge length) on TopCoW MR, TopCoW CT, AortaSeg24 and ImageCAS, which is $8.1\tau$, $4.8\tau$, $3.1\tau$ and $2.3\tau$, against \SI{0.28}{}, \SI{0.28}{}, \SI{0.50}{} and \SI{0.27}{\milli\metre} for the references. Only this spacing was taken from TopoVST, not its predictions.}
+
+\subsection{Controlled error injection}
+
+Because no ground-truth ranking of reconstructions exists, a ranking was generated by injecting controlled errors of known size, as in previous studies \citep{citraro2020reliable,decroocq2025benchmarking}. Each reference graph was degraded by an error of known type and size, and each metric was evaluated by how well it tracked that size. The error taxonomy followed \citet{decroocq2025benchmarking}, who grouped vascular reconstruction errors into topology, morphology and geometry, restricted to errors that are well posed on a graph. The ten errors are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:error-taxonomy}.
+
+
+\begin{figure}[t]
+\centering
+\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{napake2.png}
+\caption{The ten injected structural errors on one vessel tree. Green: centerline; yellow: nodes; red: added or moved structure; red dashed: removed structure; blue cross: severed connection.}
+\label{fig:error-taxonomy}
+\end{figure}
+
+\textit{Topology.} Disconnection cut a branch in two\rev{; where the branch lies on a cycle, the cut opens the cycle instead of separating the graph}. False component added a detached fragment. False loop duplicated a stretch of vessel as a second parallel arc. Cycle disconnection severed an existing cycle and was therefore applied only where $\bone > 0$\rev{; each cut removed a contiguous stretch of one cycle between its bifurcations, and was kept only if it lowered $\bone$ by one and left $\bzero$ unchanged}. \textit{Morphology.} Missing branch pruned terminal branches. False branch added spurious branches. Spurious bifurcation added a short stub partway along a branch. Bifurcation shift moved a bifurcation along one of its branches\rev{, by exactly \SI{3}{\milli\metre} of arc length along a branch chosen at random among those longer than \SI{3.25}{\milli\metre}. The branch that continues the same vessel through the old bifurcation kept the old junction node, which became an ordinary point of the curve; the other branches were re-attached at the new position, their interior nodes translated by an amount that tapers from the full displacement to zero, so that no endpoint or other bifurcation moved}. \textit{Geometry.} Deformation applied a smooth sinusoidal warp. Jitter added independent Gaussian noise to every node. Jitter is not part of the source taxonomy, which operates on masks, but it models centerline localisation error on a graph.
+
+Each perturbation was applied at six severity steps\rev{: 1, 2, 3, 4, 6 and 8 errors for the errors that are counted, and an amplitude or standard deviation of 0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1.0 and \SI{1.5}{\milli\metre} for deformation and jitter. Table~\ref{tab:severity} gives the steps, the size of one counted error and every size in units of $\tau$}. A severity step defines the requested error, not the realised one: pruning eight branches removes a different length in every case. Each run therefore recorded the \textit{realised} error size: \rev{the change in total edge length for the errors that add or remove structure, which includes the edges joining an added or removed piece to the graph; the summed displacement of the moved bifurcations for bifurcation shift;} and the mean node displacement for geometric errors. Sensitivity was measured against this realised error size. \rev{Every run also recorded the change in $\bzero$ and $\bone$, and for bifurcation shift whether any endpoint or other bifurcation had moved. A shift that would have moved one, changed a Betti number or made two nodes coincide was rejected, and the next bifurcation was tried.}
+
