+\subsection{\rev{Two controls}}
+
+\rev{\textit{Controlled junction displacement.} In the error sweep, the severity of a bifurcation shift is the number of bifurcations moved, each by \SI{3}{\milli\metre}, so the sweep does not show whether a metric resolves how far a bifurcation moved. In 90 cases, 10 synthetic and 20 each of TopCoW MR, TopCoW CT, AortaSeg24 and ImageCAS, chosen at quantiles of the node count and with the same patients for both TopCoW sets, one bifurcation and one host branch longer than \SI{6.25}{\milli\metre} were fixed, and the bifurcation was moved along it by 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1, 1.5, 2, 3, 4, 5 and \SI{6}{\milli\metre} of arc.}
+
+\rev{\textit{Numerical variation.} A response is only meaningful if it exceeds the numerical error of the metric on graphs that are not identical. On the same 90 cases, every metric was computed after a deformation and a jitter of \SI{0.1}{\milli\metre}, and again after splitting every edge into two or three collinear pieces, after permuting the node indices, and with the distance profiles integrated to $\tau/40$ and $\tau/100$ instead of $\tau/10$. The same variants were applied at the three smallest junction displacements.}
+
+\subsection{Sensitivity score}
+
+The sensitivity of each metric to each error was quantified by a score that combines two properties: whether the metric orders the severities correctly, and whether adjacent severities can be distinguished given the variation between cases. For a metric $m$ and error $e$, the score was defined as
+\begin{equation}
+S(m, e) = \max(0, \rho) \cdot \min\!\Big(\frac{d}{4}, 1\Big),
+\label{eq:score}
+\end{equation}
+where $\rho$ is the Spearman rank correlation \citep{spearman1904} between the metric's shift and the realised error size, and $d$ is the median Cohen's $d$ \citep{cohen1988} between adjacent severity steps. Both terms were required, because with six severities almost every responsive metric reached $\rho = 0.9$. Negative $\rho$ was set to zero. The effect size was capped at $d = 4$, \rev{at which three cases per group separate two adjacent severities with 80\% power in a two-sided $t$-test \citep{cohen1988} (two cases per group give 56\%); above the cap the separation was taken as complete. Adjacent severities whose shifts were the same constant, typically zero because the metric did not respond between them, counted as $d = 0$. The cap is a convention that sets how strongly the score weights the separation between cases, and the selected metrics depend on it, so every result was also computed with caps of 2 and 1, at which six and seventeen cases per group are needed.}
+
+\rev{$S$ therefore mixes two properties that can disagree. A metric can order the severities perfectly within every case, a larger error always giving a larger shift, and still separate adjacent severities poorly across cases, when its response varies more between patients than between steps. The two were therefore also reported separately: the \textit{within-case ordering}, the share of pairs of severity steps of one case with different realised sizes in which the larger error gave the larger shift (a tie counting one half), averaged within each case and then over cases; and the separation, $d$ itself. A metric was taken to respond when its shift exceeded $10^{-9}$, below which a shift is numerical noise. Note that $\rho$ uses the realised size whereas $d$ compares the requested severity steps, and that for the errors that add or remove structure the realised size is a length, so a metric that counts events, such as endpoint F1, is ranked against length. Note also that $d$ compares adjacent steps across cases, although each case is its own control; a paired effect size, the mean change between adjacent steps within a case divided by its standard deviation, was computed as an alternative. An error was scored on a dataset only if it formed a severity ladder there: the median case had to show at least two distinct responses of total length, rounded to $10^{-4}$\,mm, over the six steps. Cycle disconnection on TopCoW fails this rule and is marked n/s.}
+
+Some metric--error pairs were circular\rev{: the metric is, by definition, the quantity the error injects, so it reads the perturbation back rather than measuring the reconstruction. Total length is the sum of the edge lengths, and disconnection, false component, false loop, cycle disconnection, missing branch, false branch and spurious bifurcation only add or remove edges, so its shift equals the realised error size against which it is correlated. $\bzero$ error counts the components that false component and disconnection add, and $\bone$ error the cycles that false loop adds and cycle disconnection removes. These eleven pairs were identified from the definitions. Identifying circular pairs by zero variance of the shift within severity steps, the obvious alternative, misses most of them: the length of a false component is drawn from $\pm 25\%$ around \SI{5}{\milli\metre}, precisely so that $d$ stays defined, and that spread carries straight into total length.} They were retained in the tables, where they are marked with $\dagger$, but excluded from the metric-set selection. A dash in the tables marks a metric that did not respond to an error at all. This differs from a score of $0.00$, which indicates that the metric responded but ordered the severities no better than chance.
+
+\subsection{Redundancy and metric-set selection}
+
+Selection of a \rev{compact} metric set required two steps: the identification of redundant metrics and the determination of the number of metrics required to cover all error types. Two metrics were considered redundant when their responses to all errors were similar. For each metric, a response profile was constructed from its mean shift at every combination of error and severity, normalised to the maximum of the profile. Pairwise Pearson correlations between the profiles were then computed. Because every responsive metric increases with error size, all profiles share a common trend, which dominates the correlation independently of what each metric measures. The mean across metrics was therefore subtracted at each combination of error and severity before correlation. Without this correction, the correlation reflected error severity rather than agreement between metrics, and the resulting clusters were not reproducible across datasets. The common trend was taken from the main metrics only, so that the node-based metrics, which were added to the analysis for comparison, did not change the correlations among the others. \rev{Groups of metrics were formed by average-linkage hierarchical clustering on one minus the correlation averaged over the five datasets, cut at a distance of 0.3. Each profile is normalised to its maximum, so the errors with the largest shifts, jitter above all, dominate it; a high correlation therefore means a similar pattern of response, not equal scores.}
+
+\rev{Metric sets were selected on the sensitivity scores of the five datasets pooled.} The coverage of an error was defined as the highest score reached by any selected metric, so that a metric strong on an already covered error contributed no additional coverage. \rev{The objective was the coverage summed over every combination of dataset and error on which the error forms a severity ladder, 46 in all, and the best set of three metrics was found by exhaustive search over all 969 triplets of the nineteen metrics. Coverage is a monotone submodular objective, for which greedy selection, adding at each step the metric with the largest increase in summed coverage, is guaranteed only a fraction $1 - 1/e$ of the optimum \citep{nemhauser1978analysis}; with nineteen metrics the exhaustive search is cheap, and the greedy order, stopped when the increase fell below $0.05$, is reported alongside it.} Circular metric--error pairs contributed zero coverage, and the node-based metrics took no part in the selection.
+
+\rev{The stability of the selection was assessed with 1000 bootstrap replicates. In each, the cases of every dataset were drawn with replacement, all severities of a drawn case kept together, and the whole score matrix and selection were recomputed. TopCoW MR and TopCoW CT image the same patients, so the two were drawn together, by patient; drawing either modality alone gave the same results. The selection was also repeated with each dataset held out, with both TopCoW datasets held out together, and with each error family held out. The set chosen on the rest was scored on the held-out targets, relative to the best triplet chosen on those targets themselves.}
+
+\subsection{Implementation}
+
+All metrics, perturbations and the sweep harness were implemented in \ceval, a Python package built on NumPy \citep{harris2020numpy} and SciPy \citep{virtanen2020scipy}, released at \url{https://github.com/zbizjak/cEval}. The package includes a Dockerfile that pins the full software stack. Every value is returned with the parameters that produced it.
