+\new{At the node spacing of TopoVST, node F1 on graphs identical to the reference was $0.54$ on TopCoW MR, $0.72$ on TopCoW CT, $0.86$ on AortaSeg24 and $1.00$ on ImageCAS, where TopoVST's spacing is closest to $2\tau$. Chamfer distance was \SI{0.59}{}, \SI{0.47}{}, \SI{0.59}{} and \SI{0.19}{\milli\metre}. \rev{A prediction that traced the reference curve exactly with TopoVST's node spacing} would therefore be reported with a node F1 of $0.54$ on TopCoW MR, the score of a poor reconstruction, by the metric and not by the method. \rev{The reference in this test is itself the chorded curve, so the test isolates node placement; on a curved vessel, a prediction with straight edges every \SI{5.7}{\milli\metre} would also depart from the vessel, by 0.4 to \SI{0.9}{\milli\metre} where the radius of curvature is 5 to \SI{10}{\milli\metre} (0.6 to $1.3\tau$ on TopCoW MR), an error of discretisation that comes on top.} The eight metrics of the main set that were moved to the curve behaved in the same way when they were computed on the nodes (Figure~\ref{fig:spacing-nodes}): the scores fell beyond a node step of about $2\tau$, the two branch coverage scores to near $0$, and ASCD and HD95 grew with the node step.}
+
+\subsection{\rev{Numerical error}}
+
+\rev{The node spacing changes node-based scores although the reconstruction is the same; numerical error does not change the curve metrics appreciably. On graphs deformed or jittered by \SI{0.1}{\milli\metre}, splitting edges, permuting node indices and refining the integration from $\tau/10$ to $\tau/100$ changed NetMets geom.\ F1 by at most 0.6\% of its response in the settings tested, ASCD by at most 0.25\% and HD95 by at most 2.5\%, and left APLS, the structural counts and the descriptors unchanged to floating-point precision. At the three smallest junction displacements, 0.25 to \SI{0.75}{\milli\metre}, the numerical variation of NetMets geom.\ F1 and ASCD was in the median two to three orders of magnitude below their response. In the worst case the response exceeded the numerical range 3.1-fold for NetMets geom.\ F1 and 5.2-fold for ASCD, and the change between adjacent displacements exceeded the two ranges together 1.9-fold and 1.04-fold; the values of adjacent displacements never overlapped. The grading of small displacements reported below is therefore resolved for the settings tested, in the worst case by a narrow margin.}
+
+\subsection{\rev{The injected errors}}
+
+\rev{Every perturbation was checked against what it was asked to do (Table~\ref{tab:validation}). Every false component and false loop changed $\bzero$ or $\bone$ by exactly the number applied, every cycle disconnection lowered $\bone$ by exactly that number without separating a component, and no morphological or geometric error changed a Betti number. A shifted bifurcation moved no endpoint or other bifurcation in any of the 8280 runs; 113 shifts that would have done so, or would have made two nodes coincide, were rejected, and the realised arc length matched the requested \SI{3}{\milli\metre} to within \SI{1e-6}{\milli\metre}. Disconnection opened a cycle instead of separating the graph in 607 of 33{,}048 cuts; 306 of these were among the 720 cuts on the synthetic set, whose graphs carry six cycles each, so that on this set disconnection is in part a cycle error. Where a case had fewer eligible sites than a severity step requested, fewer errors were applied, most often for bifurcation shift (1074 runs, almost all on ImageCAS and TopCoW, where fewer bifurcations have a branch longer than \SI{3.25}{\milli\metre}); sensitivity was measured against the number actually applied.}
