+No metric reported every error. \rev{Which metrics were insensitive to an error depended mostly on what each family counts, but for the tolerance-based metrics also on the size of the error relative to $\tau$ (Table~\ref{tab:severity}): on AortaSeg24, where $\tau = \SI{1.6}{\milli\metre}$, the strongest deformation, \SI{1.5}{\milli\metre} or $0.94\tau$, stays within the tolerance band, and none of the seven hard-threshold metrics, Edge F1, Edge R, overlap, both branch coverage scores, junction F1 and endpoint F1, responded to deformation.} The errors are described in turn below, with those that behaved alike grouped together. \textit{A cut vessel or an added fragment} (disconnection, false component) was the case the metrics detected most reliably \rev{outside ImageCAS. For disconnection, the strongest non-circular metric reached 0.979 on the synthetic reference (ASCD) and 0.905, 0.739 and 0.966 on TopCoW MR, TopCoW CT and AortaSeg24 (endpoint F1), but 0.383 on ImageCAS. Total length and $\bzero$ error would score highest, but they count the cut back and are circular; with $\bzero$ error counted, ImageCAS would reach 0.972, and no selected set below would change}. A false component was reported by endpoint F1 at $0.94$ or more on the three clinical datasets other than ImageCAS. On ImageCAS, whose references consist of \new{two trees, one per coronary artery}, the best metric other than the circular $\bzero$ error was \new{endpoint F1, at $0.40$}. Both errors change the number of connected components, so $\bzero$ error reported them\rev{; it is the direct measure of either, and it is left out of the scores only because it counts back what was injected}.
+
+\textit{Added or removed branches} (missing branch, false branch, spurious bifurcation) were detected best by endpoint F1, because adding or pruning a branch adds or removes an endpoint. Endpoint F1 was the strongest metric for \new{all three errors on all five datasets}, with scores between $0.92$ and $0.99$ \new{on the four datasets other than ImageCAS}. On ImageCAS a missing branch reached only \new{$0.320$}, and a false branch and a spurious bifurcation \new{$0.396$ each}. \rev{Endpoint F1 ordered these errors within every ImageCAS case without error; its scores there are low because the size of its response varied between cases (see Sensitivity by dataset).} False branch and spurious bifurcation behaved almost identically, and their scores\rev{, over all metrics and datasets,} correlated at $0.94$.
+
+\textit{A false loop} was the only error to which $\bone$ error responded, with scores between \rev{$0.98$} and $0.99$ on all five datasets\rev{; the score is circular on all five, because adding $k$ arcs raises the cycle count by exactly $k$. Among the other metrics, the best was branch coverage F1 (80\%) on the synthetic reference (0.974), the branch count ratio on AortaSeg24 (0.864), ASCD on TopCoW MR (0.735) and ImageCAS (0.320), and Edge F1 on TopCoW CT (0.679). After bifurcation shift, a false loop was the error the metrics separated least well across cases.}
+
+\rev{\textit{A displaced bifurcation} (bifurcation shift) adds and removes nothing and leaves every count unchanged, so all three structural counts returned a dash, and so did endpoint F1, whose endpoints do not move. It had the lowest best score of any error, 0.620 on average and 0.336 on ImageCAS, reached by junction F1 on the synthetic reference (0.975) and AortaSeg24 (0.785) and by branch coverage F1 on the other three datasets. The low score does not mean that the metrics missed it. ASCD, NetMets geom.\ F1, branch coverage F1 and junction F1 responded in every run and ordered the severities within a case almost without error (within-case ordering 0.998 to 1.000 on every dataset), and their Spearman correlation with the realised displacement was 0.82 to 0.97. What they did not do was separate adjacent severities across cases: $d$ was 0.9 to 2.6, except for junction F1 on the synthetic reference (5.9) and AortaSeg24 (3.2), so the cap of 4 roughly halved their scores; at caps of 2 and 1 the mean best score was 0.908 and 0.958. In the controlled sweep, in which one bifurcation was moved by 0.25 to \SI{6}{\milli\metre} of arc length along its host branch, and the response was ordered by the realised straight-line displacement (Figure~\ref{fig:junction}, Table~\ref{tab:junction}), ASCD increased strictly with the realised displacement in all 90 cases and NetMets geom.\ F1 in 88 (lowest within-case $\rho = 0.96$; in the other two, the responses to the smallest displacements were below $10^{-9}$ or decreased by $10^{-5}$), and APLS nearly did. Junction F1 acted as a step detector: it did not respond below about $\tau$ and responded by a constant amount above it. Branch coverage F1 likewise did not respond below $\tau$, and HD95 and endpoint F1 did not respond at all.}
