+\subsection{Redundancy}
+
+Figure~\ref{fig:redundancy} shows the correlation between every pair of response profiles, after the trend they all share was removed. Most correlations were low, so most metrics were not substitutes for one another, and the nineteen metrics separated into fourteen groups \rev{(average-linkage clustering of the correlations averaged over the five datasets)} at a correlation cut of $0.3$. Two pairs of metrics correlated above $0.9$ on all five datasets. Edge F1 and overlap differ only in how they combine the two covered lengths, and they coincide whenever the covered lengths of the two graphs are equal. Total length and tortuosity both grow with the added length of the curve. Among the node-based metrics, Node F1 behaved like overlap and Chamfer distance like ASCD on all five datasets: on graphs of comparable node spacing they measure the same quantities, but with the node placement added (\new{Figure~\ref{fig:spacing}}). \rev{A high correlation of the profiles means a shared pattern of response, not equal scores: on disconnection, total length scored 1.000 on the synthetic reference and both TopCoW sets, where tortuosity scored 0.07 to 0.12. Two metrics of such a pair answer the same question, and reporting both adds little. Figure~\ref{fig:redundancy} shows the synthetic dataset; the pairs and groups quoted hold on every dataset.}
