+\subsection{\rev{A compact metric set}}
+
+A small set of metrics was sufficient. Selecting a separate set for each dataset would not help a reader who does not know which of these five datasets their own data resembles. A single set was therefore chosen on the five datasets pooled \rev{(Figure~\ref{fig:greedy-coverage}, Table~\ref{tab:cap}). The best of all 969 triplets was ASCD, endpoint F1 and junction F1, which reached 98.5\% of the coverage that all nineteen metrics achieve together: 99.9\% on the synthetic reference, 98.9\% and 99.2\% on TopCoW MR and CT, 95.9\% on AortaSeg24 and 98.7\% on ImageCAS. The greedy heuristic picks NetMets geom.\ F1 first, then endpoint F1 and junction F1, and reaches 97.9\%; having taken NetMets geom.\ F1, it cannot reach the ASCD triplet. Most of the coverage came from two metrics: NetMets geom.\ F1 and endpoint F1 alone reached 90 to 99\% on every dataset, and junction F1 added 1.16 of an error, against 6.24 for endpoint F1. Total length was in neither triplet: its strongest responses are to the errors for which it is circular, and NetMets geom.\ F1, endpoint F1 and total length together reached 95.2\%. It remains a valid descriptor of how much centerline a reconstruction has; it is left out here only because those responses read the injected length back.}
+
+\input{tables_v5/tables_holdout}
+
+\rev{The choice was stable across cases. In the bootstrap, endpoint F1 together with ASCD or NetMets geom.\ F1 was part of the best triplet in all 1000 replicates, and the best triplet was ASCD, endpoint F1 and junction F1 in 995; total length never entered it. The best triplet reached 0.984 of the available coverage (95\% interval 0.979--0.988), against 0.954 (0.944--0.961) for NetMets geom.\ F1, endpoint F1 and total length. Held out, the choice transferred (Table~\ref{tab:holdout}): a triplet chosen greedily on four datasets reached 94.2 to 99.3\% of the best triplet chosen on the fifth, and 98.5\% when both TopCoW sets were held out together. NetMets geom.\ F1, endpoint F1 and junction F1 were chosen in every fold except the one that held out the synthetic reference, where branch coverage F1 replaced junction F1. Holding out a family of errors cost more: a set chosen without the morphology errors reached 91.1\% on them, and one chosen without the topology errors 96.3\%. A benchmark meant to cover a family of errors has to include it.}
+
+\rev{Reporting endpoint F1 with ASCD or NetMets geom.\ F1, and junction F1 at this cap, therefore captured most of the coverage.} These metrics detect that an error is present, but they do not identify it: \rev{a lower endpoint F1 is reported whether a branch was lost or a false one added}. We therefore recommend reporting, alongside \rev{them}, the metrics that measure such a quantity directly. $\bzero$ error counts the connected components, and so states whether a vessel was severed or a false fragment added; $\bone$ error counts the cycles, and so states whether a circle of Willis was closed or opened; the branch count ratio describes the branching pattern; branch coverage F1 states whether a vessel is traced as one branch or several. Each of these responded to few errors and stayed near zero on the rest, which is why none of them was among the first three selected for coverage, and also why each answers a question the three cannot. \rev{Bifurcation shift remained the weakest error: the best triplet reached 0.583 on it on average, and no fourth metric raised this by more than 0.04 (branch coverage F1).}
+
+\subsection{\rev{Dependence on the score}}
+
+\rev{The cap of 4 on $d$ decides how strongly the score weights the separation between cases, and the selected set depends on it (Table~\ref{tab:cap}). With caps of 2 and 1, the best triplet was NetMets geom.\ F1, endpoint F1 and branch coverage F1, with 99.9\% of the coverage at both; at a cap of 2, ASCD replaced NetMets geom.\ F1 in 40\% of the bootstrap replicates. At every cap, endpoint F1 together with ASCD or NetMets geom.\ F1 was part of the best triplet in all 1000 replicates, and total length in none. The third metric is the part of the set that depends on the convention: junction F1 when the separation between cases is weighted strongly, branch coverage F1 when it is not. It added 1.16 of an error at caps of 4 and 2, and only 0.14 at a cap of 1.}
+
+\input{tables_v5/tables_cap}
+
+\rev{The cap changed the best score of every error whose strongest metric separates adjacent severities by less than $d = 4$ (Table~\ref{tab:cap-errors}). For disconnection, the mean best score rose from 0.794 at a cap of 4 to 0.935 at 2 and 0.965 at 1; on TopCoW the best metric changed from endpoint F1 to overlap and NetMets geom.\ F1, and on ImageCAS endpoint F1 stayed best but rose from 0.383 to 0.766 and 0.919. For a false component the mean rose from 0.856 to 0.952 and 0.980, endpoint F1 staying best on the clinical datasets. For a false loop it rose from 0.715 to 0.913 and 0.973, and at caps of 2 and 1 ASCD was the best metric on every dataset, where at a cap of 4 four different metrics were. For a missing branch it rose from 0.818 to 0.897 and 0.984; endpoint F1 was best everywhere at a cap of 4, but at a cap of 1 NetMets geom.\ F1 or Edge R overtook it on four datasets. False branch and spurious bifurcation rose from 0.869 to 0.949 and 0.979, endpoint F1 staying best at every cap and only the ImageCAS scores changing (0.396, 0.792 and 0.945). For bifurcation shift the mean best score rose from 0.620 to 0.908 and 0.958 with the same metrics, junction F1 and branch coverage F1, staying best; on TopCoW it nearly doubled, from 0.50 to 0.96. Cycle disconnection, deformation and jitter did not change, because their best metrics separate adjacent severities by more than $d = 4$.}
+
+\rev{The order of the errors changed less than their scores. Bifurcation shift was the weakest error at caps of 4 (0.620) and 1 (0.958); at a cap of 2 a missing branch was lower (0.897 against 0.908), and in the bootstrap it was the weakest in 82\% of replicates. ImageCAS changed most for every error: its mean best score was 0.502, 0.786 and 0.948 at the three caps. Its responses vary between cases more than elsewhere, so $d$ is small and the cap decides the score (see Sensitivity by dataset).}
+
+\rev{The definition of circular pairs mattered as much as the cap. With circular pairs identified by zero variance of the shift instead, total length was in the best triplet in 99 to 100\% of replicates at every cap, and endpoint F1 dropped out of it in 17\% and 41\% of replicates at caps of 2 and 1 (Table~\ref{tab:cap}); endpoint F1's place in the core holds only with the circular pairs identified from their definitions. The effect size mattered too. With the paired effect size, the mean best score rose for every error whose $d$ was below the cap, from 0.620 to 0.854 for bifurcation shift, which stayed the weakest error, and from 0.715 to 0.963 for a false loop; the best triplet at a cap of 4 became ASCD, endpoint F1 and branch coverage F1, again with endpoint F1 and a curve-distance metric as its core.}
+
+\subsection{Sensitivity by dataset}
+
+For each error, the best score any metric reached on a dataset was taken, and these scores were averaged over the errors, circular pairs excluded. The mean was \rev{0.978} on the synthetic dataset, \rev{0.884} on TopCoW MR, \rev{0.858} on TopCoW CT, \rev{0.947} on AortaSeg24 and \rev{0.502} on ImageCAS. ImageCAS was therefore the hardest dataset. \rev{Seven errors accounted for most of its lower mean: the best score was 0.320 for missing branch and false loop, 0.336 for bifurcation shift, 0.383 for disconnection, 0.396 for spurious bifurcation and false branch, and 0.402 for false component.} \rev{The metrics did not respond less within ImageCAS cases: endpoint F1 ordered disconnection, false component, missing branch and false branch within every case without error, with $\rho$ of 0.92 to 0.95, as on the other datasets. What was lower was the separation between cases, $d$ of 1.4 to 1.7 against 3 to 10 elsewhere: ImageCAS is the largest and most varied dataset, and the unpaired $d$ compares its cases with each other. With the paired effect size, its mean best score was 0.867 instead of 0.502 (Table~\ref{tab:cap}).}
+
+\subsection{\rev{Artefacts in the reference graphs}}
+
+\new{Replacing the ImageCAS references (see DATA) removed 1532 loops, 639 detached pieces and 5284 endpoints while changing the total centerline length by only $1.2\%$, and it changed the results. The sweep run on the distributed graphs had given a mean best score on ImageCAS of $0.613$, against $0.642$ on the corrected references. The scores for errors that add or remove a branch rose the most: from $0.234$ to $0.320$ for a missing branch, from $0.355$ to $0.396$ for a false branch and from $0.345$ to $0.402$ for a false component, while the coverage of the recommended set fell from $95\%$ to $91\%$. That earlier run also integrated distances to $\tau/100$ and drew the APLS pairs in node order, but these two differences changed no number on the other four datasets. Artefacts that carry their own endpoints and bifurcations dilute an injected error among them, so the sensitivity of a metric is measured against the reference as much as against the error. The extraction that replaced them needed manual correction in 413 of the 1000 cases, so an automatically extracted reference is not free of this problem either, and a reference should be checked for loops, detached fragments and short spurs before it is used.}
+
+% ============================================================================
+\section{DISCUSSION}
+% ============================================================================
+
+Centerline graphs are evaluated in different ways across the literature. The reported metrics differ between papers, the tolerance required by several of them is often not stated, and the node spacing of the graphs is rarely given. Our results show three ways in which these choices affect the reported score. First, no metric responded to every error, and \rev{the metrics of each family missed different errors}. The selected metrics therefore determine which errors an evaluation can detect, and an error to which none of them responds appears as a good score rather than as a failure. Second, two pairs of metrics behaved identically on all five datasets, and several metrics in use measure the same quantity under another name, so two reported metrics may provide only one kind of evidence. Third, metrics defined on nodes changed when only the node spacing changed and the reconstruction did not, while the same quantities computed on the curve did not change at all. As a result, two scores published under the same metric name may not measure the same thing, which limits comparison between studies.
+
+The first finding concerns the choice of metrics. The metric sets used in recent challenges were each chosen for a specific clinical question. TopCoW ranks on Dice, clDice, HD95 and $\bzero$ error, together with detection and variant-classification scores \citep{yang2023topcow}. $\bzero$ error is appropriate for the circle of Willis, where a severed vessel is the most relevant failure, and our results confirm that a component count describes such a failure more directly than any other metric we tested. However, it responds only to errors that change the number of components\rev{, and HD95, the other graph-level distance of that set, did not respond to a single displaced bifurcation in our controlled sweep}. Our coverage analysis addresses this by identifying which metrics complement it. ATM'22 adds tree-length and branch-detected ratios to Dice and precision \citep{zhang2023atm22}, which covers the loss of airway branches well, but the 80\% branch-detection rule, unlike branch coverage F1, accepts a branch as found whether the prediction traces it as one branch or as three.
+
+The second finding concerns redundancy. Some of it is a matter of naming rather than of design, because the same quantity has been reached from different directions and given different names (Table~\ref{tab:similar}). The completeness and correctness of \citet{wiedemann1998empirical}, the tree length detected of ATM'22 and the graph form of clDice are all the recall, the precision or the F1 of one covered length, Edge F1; on a graph, $\bzero$ \textit{is} the number of connected components. Edge F1 and the CAT08 overlap differ only in how they combine the two covered lengths, and they behaved identically on all five datasets. Definitions alone do not settle which names denote one quantity, since the branch count ratio and $\bone$ error are constructed alike and respond to different errors; the response profiles settle it, which is the argument for comparing metrics empirically rather than by inspection. \citet{citraro2020reliable} reached the same conclusion for road networks by a different route. Two metrics of one group should therefore not be presented as independent evidence.
+
+The third finding concerns how the metrics are computed. A centerline graph describes a curve, and its nodes are only where the extraction method chose to place them; two graphs of the same vessel can carry node counts that differ by an order of magnitude. Any metric that compares nodes inherits that choice. In the node placement test the prediction traced exactly the curve of the reference, yet Node F1 fell \new{to $0.50$ at a node step of $8\tau$ and Chamfer distance grew linearly with the step, and at the node spacing of a published method, TopoVST, a prediction tracing the reference curve of a circle of Willis exactly at that spacing would score a node F1 of $0.54$}. A reader given such a score cannot distinguish it from a poor reconstruction, and since the node spacing was rarely stated in the papers we reviewed, a published score of this kind is difficult to interpret. Computing the same quantities on the curve removes the problem rather than correcting for it. The covered length of an edge is an exact interval, distances are measured to the curve, and path lengths start and end on points of the curve, so the node placement never enters the result: all nineteen metrics of the main set returned their perfect value at every node step. On graphs of comparable node spacing the node-based metrics measure the same thing as their curve counterparts, as the correlations show (Node F1 with overlap, Chamfer distance with ASCD), but only the curve versions give the same number for the same curve. The tolerance is recorded with every value, so that a score carries the condition under which it was obtained.
+
+These results suggest selecting metrics differently rather than adding new ones. A set should be assembled so that its members fail on different errors, which is what \rev{endpoint F1 and a curve-distance metric, ASCD or NetMets geom.\ F1, do: they come from different groups and together reach most of the coverage that any combination of metrics achieves, at every setting of the score we tried}. A set of this kind reports that the reconstruction is wrong, but not in what way, and this is the limit of what a general set can do. Identifying the failure requires a metric built for the quantity in question, and the structural counts serve this purpose. Their narrow response, which makes them unsuitable as general measures, makes each of them informative in a report: $\bzero$ error states that a vessel was severed or a false fragment added, $\bone$ error that a circle of Willis was closed or opened, the branch count ratio that the branching pattern changed, branch coverage F1 that a vessel is traced in pieces. We therefore recommend reporting a general set together with whichever counts the clinical question depends on, rather than choosing between the two. \rev{A displaced bifurcation is the error a general set handles least well. The curve-distance metrics grade it within a case, but a bifurcation moved by a few millimetres changes a whole-graph average little compared with the differences between patients, so adjacent severities separate poorly. Junction F1 is computed at each bifurcation, but it only reports whether a matched bifurcation lies within $\tau$, so it is blind below the tolerance and saturates above it. A graded local measure, the distance between matched bifurcations, would combine the two, and none of the metrics we tested is of that kind.}
+
+\rev{The construction of the score mattered as much as the data. Stability across resampled cases is not robustness to how the score is built: with circular pairs identified by zero variance and a cap of 4, the greedy heuristic returned NetMets geom.\ F1, endpoint F1 and total length in all 1000 bootstrap replicates, yet that set was never the best triplet, the heuristic returned it in only 44\% of replicates at a cap of 2, and with the circular pairs identified from their definitions neither it nor the best triplet under zero-variance exclusion was selected at any cap. A paired effect size, which respects that each case is its own control, changed the third metric as well. A compact metric set is a recommendation conditional on the objective, the score and the errors it was chosen for, and should be reported with them; the part of ours that did not depend on these choices is endpoint F1 with one curve-distance metric.}
+
+To make these comparisons possible, we implemented the metrics in one evaluation tool, \ceval, so that they are computed under a single protocol and on the same graphs. Metrics that could not be computed are listed in Table~\ref{tab:not-computed} with the reason. Most need information that a centerline graph does not carry: a vessel radius, a designated root, a mask or several annotators. TOPO, the standard connectivity metric for road networks \citep{biagioni2012topo,he2020sat2graph}, would have needed an estimated 21{,}000 CPU hours for this study. The package, the perturbations and the sweep that produced these results are released at \url{https://github.com/zbizjak/cEval}, with a Dockerfile that pins the full software stack.
+
+The main limitation is that the errors were injected rather than observed, so we cannot show that they are distributed as real reconstruction failures are. The reference graphs are our only ground truth, so extraction quality is excluded rather than measured. \new{The ImageCAS references were reviewed by a single reader, and the reference artefacts we removed show that a reference can bias the measured sensitivity; \rev{the TopCoW references were used as provided and the AortaSeg24 references as annotated, without a second review}.} \rev{The recommendation is conditional on the coverage objective and on the construction of the score; we have shown how it depends on the cap and on the exclusion of circular pairs, but another objective, such as weighting errors by clinical importance, could select differently. Errors were injected one at a time, so errors that cancel in a global quantity, such as a missing and a false branch of equal length, were not tested, and no ranking of real reconstructions was available against which to check the recommendation.} The tolerance is tied to the image resolution rather than to the anatomy\rev{, and it was not varied: junction F1, the third metric at a cap of 4, responds only to displacements beyond it, and the \SI{3}{\milli\metre} bifurcation shift of the sweep lies at 1.9 to 4.3$\tau$}; a radius-adaptive tolerance in the manner of CAT08 would reflect the size of each vessel, but requires a radius channel that a centerline graph does not carry. One error, cycle disconnection, could be graded only on the synthetic reference, because the circle of Willis carries at most two cycles and the aortic and coronary trees none, so how metrics respond to it on clinical data remains untested. \rev{The strongest deformation reached only $0.94\tau$ on AortaSeg24, so the hard-threshold metrics did not see it there at all, and the TopCoW and AortaSeg24 references were not audited for artefacts.} Finally, Dice and clDice are computed on the mask rather than on the graph, and lie outside what we measured.
+
+We release \ceval{} so that centerline-graph metrics can be computed under one protocol, on the curves the graphs trace, with the tolerance recorded alongside every value. Using it, we asked which of nineteen metrics in current use are worth reporting. \rev{Endpoint F1 and one curve-distance metric, ASCD or NetMets geom.\ F1, cover most of what any metric detects on every dataset we tried, at every setting of the score, with junction F1 or branch coverage F1 as a third metric depending on how strongly the separation between cases is weighted}, and structural counts should be added where a study depends on the quantity they measure. Metrics defined on nodes, Node F1, Chamfer distance, false merges and false splits, report the node spacing as well as the reconstruction and should not be used to compare graphs of different discretisation. One clinically meaningful error, a displaced bifurcation, \rev{is graded within a case by the curve-distance metrics but separated poorly across cases by every metric we tested}, and we have stated what a metric would need in order to \rev{do so}.
+
